<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buto-Maadi culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th millennium BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Maadi culture, Maadi-Buto culture</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Buto-Maadi culture was the main prehistoric culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> in the 4th millennium BCE, named after the sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buto">Buto</a> in the Delta and Maadi near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. Its people lived in villages, farmed and herded, and traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, pottery and other goods with the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. It was gradually replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada">Naqada</a> culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> before the unification of the country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buto-maadi-culture.jpg" alt="Buto-Maadi culture" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Collage of:Image:Diorite Vase Neqada II Predynastic Ancient Egypt Field Museum.jpg by Madman2001, Image:Egypte louvre 322.jpg and … · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Predynastic_collage_(new_version).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
